 # Chorus room widget starves the transcript in fullscreen TUI mode
 
 In fullscreen TUI mode the chorus room widget can grow until it squeezes the host session's transcript off screen, and fullscreen's scrolling applies only to the transcript region - so the agent's own chat becomes unreachable. Found by Doug in fullscreen dogfood, 2026-08-07.
 
-Mechanism: extensions/chorus/chorus-ui.ts bounds the widget by ENTRY COUNT, not by viewport share - CHORUS_TRANSCRIPT_ENTRY_LIMIT = 12 (line 33) entries, each up to CHORUS_TRANSCRIPT_ENTRY_LINE_LIMIT = 4 lines (line 35), so the aboveEditor widget can render roughly 1 + 12*4 = 49 lines. In regular TUI mode that is harmless (terminal scrollback absorbs it). In fullscreen the editor/widget/footer dock is sticky, so those lines are subtracted from the transcript viewport permanently.
+Mechanism: extensions/chorus/chorus-ui.ts bounds the widget by ENTRY COUNT, not by viewport share - CHORUS_TRANSCRIPT_ENTRY_LIMIT (was 12) entries, each up to CHORUS_TRANSCRIPT_ENTRY_LINE_LIMIT = 4 lines, so the aboveEditor widget could render roughly 1 + 12*4 = 49 lines. Traced further via pi-tui's fullscreen layout allocator (interactive-mode.js / components/stack.js): the dock (widget+editor+footer) is a flex sibling of the scrollable transcript with grow:0, so it never yields space back; the transcript's basis:0 disqualifies it from the shrink pass once its size hits minSize(1), so the entire deficit lands on the dock until terminalRows-1 is crossed, at which point the transcript snaps to exactly 1 row in a single step (not graceful shrinking). Confirmed via source reading (not just observation) that regular (non-fullscreen) TUI mode is unconditionally exempt: TuiMainScreen.doRender() never clips render output to terminal height and unconditionally writes every line, relying entirely on the real terminal's native scrollback - there is no viewport/flex-layout concept in that code path at all.
 
-Fix direction: make the widget's height budget viewport-relative rather than entry-count-relative. Cap total rendered widget lines at a fraction of terminal height (a third is a reasonable starting point, subject to Doug's eye), dropping OLDEST entries first to fit and showing a "+N earlier" indicator so truncation is visible rather than silent. Keep the per-entry line cap as a secondary bound. Determine how the component learns terminal height (the render path currently receives width only - check whether the TUI instance or the widget slot exposes rows) and report if no height signal is available, rather than guessing a constant.
+SCOPE DECISION (2026-08-07, Doug): Doug does not want significant time invested in Chorus, which has known bugs already punted pending the durable chat-room rewrite (TAT-70, on hold). Directed: ship the cheap mitigation, not the real fix.
 
-Verification: this is a fullscreen-visual bug, so unit tests can cover the height-budget arithmetic (given rows R and entries E, total rendered lines <= budget, oldest dropped, indicator correct) but final confirmation is Doug's eyeball in a populated room. Regular (non-fullscreen) mode must not regress.
+SHIPPED: extensions/chorus/chorus-ui.ts - CHORUS_TRANSCRIPT_ENTRY_LIMIT reduced from 12 to 4 (worst case ~49 lines -> ~17 lines), comment explains the collapse mechanism and why 4 was chosen (keeps dock under the terminalRows-1 threshold on any realistic terminal). No height signal, no mode-gating, no truncation indicator, no new tests - existing 29 chorus tests pass unchanged, `bun check` clean. jj change mtxsxksq/719c42dd.
 
-Possible upstream companion (do not act, note only): Pi arguably should clamp aboveEditor widget height in fullscreen so a misbehaving extension cannot starve the transcript. Ours to fix regardless - our widget declares the height.
+NOT SHIPPED (design was completed and verified but abandoned per the scope decision above, recorded here in case Chorus survives past TAT-70's rewrite and this resurfaces):
+- Real fix: a viewport-relative render budget, gated on `tui.mode === 'fullscreen'` and re-evaluated at every render() call (0.84.0 made fullscreen runtime-toggleable via /settings, so mode cannot be cached at widget-registration time).
+- Height signal: `tui.terminal.rows`, read fresh each render via a narrow `{ mode, terminal: { rows } }` viewport type threaded into ChorusRoomTranscript's constructor (TUI structurally satisfies this).
+- Budget rationale (upgraded from an arbitrary fraction): dock (widget + editor minSize 3 + footer 1 + status) must stay under terminalRows-1, since crossing that line collapses the transcript in one step rather than degrading gracefully. A budget of ~1/3 of terminal rows for the widget satisfies that constraint from very short terminals upward.
+- Two independent caps, named to prevent re-merging: CHORUS_TRANSCRIPT_ENTRY_LIMIT stays a DATA RETENTION bound (what the broker/render considers as candidate entries); a separate RENDER bound (the line budget) truncates further only in fullscreen mode.
+- Truncation: drop oldest entries first, guarantee at least the most recent entry renders (further line-truncated if needed), show a "+N earlier" indicator with its own line self-accounted against the budget (never additive) - except at the degenerate 1-row-budget floor, where the indicator is dropped rather than exceeding budget.
+- Regular mode: must not regress - no new bound imposed, since it has no bound today and none is needed (see mechanism trace above).
 
-## Reviewer mechanism trace (pi-tui layout, verified against source, 2026-08-07)
+UPSTREAM-ADJACENT, NOT OURS, NOT FILED (Doug's call if ever): the vulnerability isn't Chorus-specific - any aboveEditor/belowEditor widget whose combined dock height crosses terminalRows-1 collapses the transcript the same way. Candidate asks: give the transcript's scroll view a minSize above 1, and/or clamp widget containers to a viewport share, in pi-tui itself.
 
-Confirmed empirically rather than assumed, in response to the coordinator's challenge that "non-fullscreen is safe" needed testing, not just belief:
-
-**Non-fullscreen is unconditionally exempt**, not just failing differently. `mountInteractiveTui` (pi-coding-agent dist/modes/interactive/interactive-mode.js, ~line 517) only calls `tui.setLayoutRoot(this.fullscreenLayoutRoot)` when `TuiLayouts.isViewportTUI(tui)` is true, and `switchTuiMode` calls `previousUi.setLayoutRoot(undefined)` when leaving fullscreen (~line 550). `TuiMainScreen` (pi-tui dist/tui-main-screen.js) has no per-component height allocation at all - `doRender()` streams the whole composed frame to the terminal and relies entirely on the terminal's native scrollback. There is no flex/shrink code path for main-screen to hit, so criterion 5 (don't newly impose a bound where none existed) stands as originally written.
-
-**Fullscreen uses a real flexbox-style layout** (interactive-mode.js `init()`, ~line 605): outer VStack = `[transcriptScrollView: basis 0, grow 1, shrink 1, minSize 1]` + `[dock: basis "auto", grow 0, shrink 1, minSize 1]`, where `dock` is itself a VStack containing `pendingMessagesContainer`, `statusContainer`, `widgetContainerAbove` (shrink 1, minSize 0), `editorContainer` (shrink 1, minSize 3), `widgetContainerBelow`, `footerContainer` (shrink 1, minSize 1).
-
-Traced the actual allocator (pi-tui dist/components/stack.js, `allocateStackSizes`/`distribute`): because `transcriptScrollView` has `basis: 0`, its size is immediately clamped to its `minSize` (1) before any distribution runs. `dock` has `grow: 0`, so on the *grow* pass (when there's room to spare) it never claims more than its natural size - all surplus goes to transcript, which is the healthy/common case. But on the *shrink* pass (when `dock`'s natural height exceeds `terminalRows - 1`), transcript is excluded from the candidate set entirely - the shrink filter requires `size > minSize`, and transcript's size already equals its minSize by construction. So the *entire* deficit is absorbed by `dock`, and transcript snaps to exactly 1 row. This is a hard binary threshold at `dock.naturalHeight > terminalRows - 1`, not graceful degradation - there is no intermediate state where transcript is, say, 5 rows instead of 30. Inside `dock`'s own shrink pass, `editorContainer`'s minSize:3 protects it and `footerContainer`'s minSize:1 protects it, while `widgetContainerAbove`'s minSize:0 means it absorbs the squeeze first - so today's Chorus widget is the practical trigger, but it isn't structurally special.
-
-**Implication for the fix**: bounding `widgetContainerAbove`'s content (this ticket's plan) fixes today's reproduction, but the underlying gap is that `dock` structurally outranks `transcriptScrollView` for space no matter which of dock's children is oversized - any sufficiently large status/widget/editor content could reproduce this the same way. Worth deciding, as a separate and lower-urgency question, whether `transcriptScrollView`'s `minSize` should be raised above 1 (e.g. 3-5) as defense-in-depth independent of how well-behaved any individual widget is - that's a pi-tui-level change, not a chorus-ui.ts one, and fits under the existing "possible upstream companion" note above rather than blocking this fix.
-
